    "explanationEn": "In Young's double-slit interference experiment, the width of one slit is half that of the other. The intensity, I , is proportional to the slit width. This gives us: I 1 = I 0 , I 2 = 2 I 0 . The maximum intensity, I max , occurs when the amplitudes add constructively: I max = ( √ I 1 + √ I 2 ) 2 . The minimum intensity, I min , occurs when the amplitudes interfere destructively: I min = ( √ I 1 − √ I 2 ) 2 . Now, using I 1 = I 0 and I 2 = 2 I 0 , we find: I max I min = ( √ 2 + 1 ) 2 ( √ 2 − 1 ) 2 = 3 + 2 √ 2 3 − 2 √ 2 .",
