    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "To determine the temperature at which the given reaction is at equilibrium, we need to apply the concept of Gibbs free energy change ( Δ G 0 ) at equilibrium, where Δ G 0 is equal to zero. The given reaction is: 1 2 X 2 + 5 2 Y 2 ⇌ XY 5 We are given the standard entropies and enthalpy change ( Δ H ⊖ ): Standard entropies: X 2 = 70 J K − 1 mol − 1 Y 2 = 50 J K − 1 mol − 1 XY 5 = 110 J K − 1 mol − 1 Standard enthalpy change: Δ H ⊖ = − 35 kJ / mol First, calculate the standard entropy change ( Δ S 0 R x n ) of the reaction: Δ S 0 R x n = 110 − ( 1 2 × 70 + 5 2 × 50 ) = 110 − ( 35 + 125 ) = 110 − 160 = − 50 J K − 1 mol − 1 At equilibrium, Δ G 0 = 0 , and the relation Δ G 0 = Δ H 0 − T Δ S 0 applies. Thus: 0 = − 35000 J / mol − T ( − 50 J K − 1 mol − 1 ) Solving for T : T ⋅ 50 = 35000 T = 35000 50 T = 700 K Therefore, the temperature at which the reaction is at equilibrium is 700 Kelvin.",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
