    "explanationEn": "When one surface of a lens is silvered, it behaves like a mirror. The effective system consists of a lens, followed by a mirror, and then the lens again (as light passes through the lens, reflects at the silvered surface, and passes back through the lens). The effective power ( P eq ) of this silvered lens is given by: P eq = 2 P l + P m Where, P l = Power of the lens and f m = Focal length of the silvered surface (mirror). The power of lens is given as P = 1 f , where focal length (f) should be in meters. The power of mirror is given as P = − 1 f , where focal length (f) should be in meters. Using the Lens Maker's Formula: 1 f l = ( μ − 1 ) ( 1 R 1 − 1 R 2 ) For a biconvex lens, the refractive index of material of lens is μ = 1.5 , the radius of curvature for the first surface is R 1 = + 20 cm (convex surface) and R 2 = − 20 cm is for the second surface (opposite convex surface) 1 f 1 = ( 1.5 − 1 ) ( 1 20 − ( 1 − 20 ) ) ⇒ 1 f 1 = 0.5 ( 1 20 + 1 20 ) = 0.5 × 2 20 = 1 20 So, the focal length of lens is f l = 20 cm . The power of lens is, P l = 1 ( 20 / 100 ) = 5 D . Since the silvered surface is the back surface of a biconvex lens, it acts as a concave mirror for the light inside the lens. So, the radius of curvature is R = − 20 cm . f m = R 2 = − 20 2 = − 10 cm The optical power of mirror is given as P m = − 1 f m , where focal length should be in meters. The power of mirror is, P m = − 1 ( − 10 / 100 ) = 10 D . Using the effective power formula, P eq = ( 2 × 5 + 10 ) D = 20 D As the effective power of the system is +20 D , and the whole system behaves like a mirror, f eq = − 1 P eq ⇒ f eq = − 1 20 m = − 100 20 cm = − 5 cm When the object is placed at the centre of curvature ( C ), the position of image and object are at the same place. So, the object distance is equal to the radius of curvature. f eq = R eq 2 ⇒ R eq = 2 × f eq = 2 × ( − 5 ) cm ⇒ R eq = − 10 cm Therefore, to have the image and object at the same place, the object should be placed at 10 cm . Hence, the correct option is (A).",
