    "explanationEn": "When a current-carrying loop is placed in an external magnetic field, it experiences a torque ( τ ⃗ ) . The magnetic properties of the loop are represented by its magnetic dipole moment ( m → ) , defined as : m → = I A → ← Where I is the current and A ⃗ is the area vector of the loop. The direction of the area vector is perpendicular to the plane of the loop, given by the unit normal n ˆ . A → = A n ˆ The torque is given by the cross product of the magnetic moment and the magnetic field : τ ⃗ = m → × B → ⇒ τ ⃗ = ( IA n ˆ ) × B → The radius of circular loop is r = 2 cm = 0.02 m . So, the area is A = π r 2 = π ( 0.02 ) 2 = 4 π × 10 − 4 m 2 The unit normal is n ˆ = k ˆ + t ˆ √ 2 And the current in loop is I = 100 √ 2 A So, the magnetic moment vector is; m → = ( 100 √ 2 ) × ( 4 π × 10 − 4 ) × ( ı ˆ + k ˆ √ 2 ) ⇒ m → = 400 π × 10 − 4 ( ı ˆ + k ˆ ) = 0.04 π ( ı ˆ + k ˆ ) A ⋅ m 2 The magnetic field in the region is B ⃗ = B o ( 3 ı ˆ + 2 k ˆ ) with B o = 4 × 10 − 3 T B → = 4 × 10 − 3 ( 3 ı ˆ + 2 k ˆ ) = ( 12 ı ˆ + 8 k ˆ ) × 10 − 3 T Using the formula for magnetic torque, τ ⃗ = m → × B → ⇒ τ ⃗ = [ 0.04 π ( ı ˆ + k ˆ ) ] × [ ( 12 ı ˆ + 8 k ˆ ) × 10 − 3 ] ⇒ τ ⃗ = ( 0.04 π × 10 − 3 ) [ ( ı ˆ + k ˆ ) × ( 12 ı ˆ + 8 k ˆ ) ] ⇒ τ ⃗ = ( 0.04 π × 10 − 3 ) ( 4 ȷ ˆ ) ⇒ τ ⃗ = 0.16 π × 10 − 3 ȷ ˆ ⇒ τ ⃗ = 0.16 × 3.14 × 10 − 3 ȷ ˆ = 0.5024 × 10 − 3 ȷ ˆ ⇒ τ ⃗ = 5024 × 10 − 7 ȷ ˆ Therefore, the torque experienced by the loop is 5024 × 10 − 7 j ˆ . Hence, the correct option is (D).",
