    "explanationEn": "According to the photoelectric effect, when light of a certain frequency or wavelength hits a metal surface, it provides energy to the electrons. Part of this energy is used to overcome the attractive forces of the metal (the work function, φ ), and the remaining energy becomes the maximum kinetic energy (K) of the emitted photoelectron. The energy of an incident photon is E = h c λ . The equation is: E = φ + K max ⇒ hc λ = φ + K max ⇒ K max = hc λ − φ We are given two different wavelengths and their corresponding kinetic energies: For wavelength λ 1 : K 1 = hc λ 1 − φ ... ( i ) For wavelength λ 2 : K 2 = hc λ 2 − φ ... ( ii ) It is given that λ 1 = 2 λ 2 . K 1 = hc 2 λ 2 − φ ... ( iii ) From equation (ii), we can find an expression for hc λ 2 : hc λ 2 = K 2 + φ Now, substituting this into equation (iii) : K 1 = 1 2 ( K 2 + φ ) − φ ⇒ 2 K 1 = K 2 + φ − 2 φ ⇒ 2 K 1 = K 2 − φ ⇒ φ = K 2 − 2 K 1 Therefore, the work function of the material is K 2 − 2 K 1 . Hence, the correct option is (D).",
