    "explanationEn": "In α -decay, a parent nucleus (X) of mass number A decays into a daughter nucleus (Y) and an α -particle (Helium nucleus, mass number 4). X A Z → Y A − 4 Z − 2 + He 4 2 Using conservation of momentum, the momentum of the α -particle ( p α ) must be equal and opposite to the momentum of the daughter nucleus ( p D ) . And using energy balance ( Q -value) the total kinetic energy released ( Q ) is shared between the α -particle and the daughter nucleus. Q = K α + K D Kinetic energy can be expressed in terms of momentum (p) and mass (m) as K = p 2 2 m . Since p α = p D , we have : K D = p 2 2 m D and K α = p 2 2 m α Therefore, K D K α = m α m D . Substituting K D = K α ( m α m D ) into the Q -value equation: Q = K α + K α ( m α m D ) = K α ( 1 + m α m D ) = K α ( m D + m α m D ) Since m D + m α ≈ A (mass number of parent) and m D ≈ A − 4 : K α = Q ( A − 4 A ) The Q-value is the same ( 1 MeV ) for both substances. For Substance A ( A A = 200 ) : K α A = Q ( 200 − 4 200 ) = Q ( 196 200 ) For Substance B ( A B = 212 ) : K α B = Q ( 212 − 4 212 ) = Q ( 208 212 ) So, the ratio is; K α A K α B = Q ( 196 200 ) Q ( 208 212 ) = 196 200 × 212 208 ⇒ K α A K α B = 49 50 × 53 52 = 49 × 53 50 × 52 = 2597 2600 Therefore, the ratio of energies of α -rays produced by A and B is 2597 2600 . Hence, the correct option is (C).",
