    "subject": "Chemistry",
    "questionEn": "The correct order of first ( Δ i H 1 ) and second ( Δ i H 2 ) ionisation enthalpy values of Cr and Mn are : A. Δ i H 1 : Cr > Mn B. Δ i H 2 : Cr > Mn C. Δ i H 1 : Mn > Cr D. Δ i H 2 : Mn > Cr Choose the correct answer from the options given below :",
    "optionsEn": [
      "A and B only",
      "B and C only",
      "A and D only",
      "C and D only"
    ],
    "correctOptionIndex": 1,
    "explanationEn": "Cr (Z = 24): Ground state configuration: Cr : [ Ar ] 3 d 5 4 s 1 (due to extra stability of half-filled 3 d 5 and 4 s 1 configuration) Mn (Z = 25): Ground state configuration: Mn : [ Ar ] 3 d 5 4 s 2 First ionisation enthalpy, Δ i H 1 In the first ionisation process: Cr: Cr → Cr + + e − After removal of one 4s electron: Cr + : [ Ar ] 3 d 5 (a very stable half-filled configuration) Mn: Mn → Mn + + e − After removal of one 4s electron: Mn + : [ Ar ] 3 d 5 4 s 1 (not as stable as half-filled 3d⁵ only) Because the resulting Cr + ion is particularly stable, less energy is required to form Cr + , i.e. Δ i H 1 ( Cr ) Δ i H 1 ( Mn ) Thus, Correct order for first ionisation enthalpy: Δ i H 1 : Mn > Cr (Corresponds to option C .) Second ionisation enthalpy, Δ i H 2 Now, let us remove the second electron. Cr: From the already stable Cr + ( [ A r ] 3 d 5 ): Cr + → Cr 2 + + e − After this, Cr 2 + becomes: [ A r ] 3 d 4 — less stable. Therefore, it requires a large amount of energy to remove this second electron. Mn: From Mn + ( [ A r ] 3 d 5 4 s 1 ): Mn + → Mn 2 + + e − After this, Mn 2 + becomes: [ A r ] 3 d 5 — a stable half-filled configuration. Thus, less energy is needed for the second ionisation. Hence, Δ i H 2 ( Cr ) > Δ i H 2 ( Mn ) which corresponds to option B . Combine results Δ i H 1 : Mn > Cr → option C Δ i H 2 : Cr > Mn → option B Therefore, the correct combination is: B and C only Final Answer: Option B",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
