    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "Statement I says: “Aluminium is more electropositive than thallium as the standard electrode potential value of E ∘ Al 3 + / Al is negative and E ∘ Tl 3 + / Tl is positive.” According to standard reduction potentials: E ∘ Al 3 + / Al = − 1.66 V E ∘ Tl 3 + / Tl = + 1.25 V A more negative standard reduction potential indicates a greater tendency to lose electrons , meaning the element is more electropositive. Therefore, aluminium (with a more negative value) is indeed more electropositive than thallium (with a positive value). So, Statement I is true . Statement II says: “The sum of the first three ionization enthalpies of boron is very high when compared to that of aluminium. Due to this reason boron forms covalent compounds only and aluminium forms Al 3 + ion.” This is correct according to periodic trends: Boron, being a small atom with high effective nuclear charge, requires very high energy to remove three electrons. Hence, it cannot easily form B 3 + ions, and thus forms covalent compounds . Aluminium, being larger in size, has lower ionization enthalpies , and can readily form Al 3 + ions in its compounds. Therefore, Statement II is also true . Both the statements are correct. Option A: Both Statement I and Statement II are true",
