    "explanationEn": "The density ( ρ ) of a body is defined as the ratio of its mass (m) to its volume (V). ρ = m V For a uniform cylinder of length l and diameter D , the volume is, V = π r 2 l = π ( D 2 ) 2 l = π D 2 l 4 Substituting the volume into the density formula: ρ = m V = 4 m π D 2 l To find the fractional error, we take the natural logarithm ( ln ) on both sides: ln ⁡ ( ρ ) = ln ⁡ ( 4 ) + ln ⁡ ( m ) − ln ⁡ ( π ) − 2 ln ⁡ ( D ) − ln ⁡ ( l ) Differentiating both sides to find the relative change d ρ ρ = dm m − 2 dD D − dl l The maximum possible fractional error is the sum of the absolute values of the individual relative errors: Δ ρ ρ = Δ m m + 2 Δ D D + Δ l l For the percentage fractional error, % Error in ρ = ( Δ m m + 2 Δ D D + Δ l l ) × 100 The measured value of mass is 97.42 ± 0.02 g ⇒ m = 97.42 g and the absolute error is Δ m = 0.02 g So, relative error in mass is, Δ m m = 0.02 97.42 ≈ 0.000205 The measured value of length is 8.35 ± 0.05 mm ⇒ l = 8.35 mm and the absolute error is Δ l = 0.05 mm So, relative error in length is, Δ l l = 0.05 8.35 ≈ 0.005988 The measured value of diameter is 20.20 ± 0.02 mm ⇒ D = 20.20 mm and the absolute error is Δ D = 0.02 mm So, relative error in diameter is, 2 Δ D D = 2 × 0.02 20.20 = 0.04 20.20 ≈ 0.001980 Putting the values in the expression for the percentage error in density, Δ ρ ρ × 100 % = ( 0.000205 + 0.005988 + 0.001980 ) × 100 % = 0.008173 × 100 % ⇒ Δ ρ ρ × 100 % = 0.8173 % ≈ 0.82 % Therefore, the calculated percentage fraction error in density is approximately 0.82 % . Hence, the correct option is (B).",
