    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "Mass defect ( Δ m ) is the difference between the sum of the masses of the individual nucleons (protons and neutrons) and the actual experimental mass of the nucleus. For the atom C 12 6 , atomic number is Z = 6 (Number of protons) Mass number is A = 12 Number of neutrons N = A − Z = 12 − 6 = 6 So, mass of 6 protons = 6 × 1.00727 u = 6.04362 u And the mass of 6 neutrons = 6 × 1.00866 u = 6.05196 u So, total theoretical mass = 6.04362 + 6.05196 = 12.09558 u It is given that the experimental mass of C 12 6 = 12 u : Hence, the mass defect is Δ m = (Total theoretical mass) − (Experimental mass) ⇒ Δ m = 12.09558 u − 12 u = 0.09558 u The energy equivalent of 1 u is given as 931.5 MeV / c 2 . Δ m ( in MeV / c 2 ) = 0.09558 × 931.5 ⇒ Δ m ≈ 89.03277 ≈ 89.03 MeV / c ∧ 2 Therefore, the mass defect of the C 12 6 atom is approximately 89.03 MeV / c 2 . Thus, the correct option is (B).",
