    "explanationEn": "For the displacement equation y = sin 2 ⁡ ω t Using the trigonometric identity sin 2 ⁡ θ = 1 − cos ⁡ 2 θ 2 . So, y = 1 2 − 1 2 cos ⁡ ( 2 ω t ) . For SHM, the acceleration must be proportional to displacement ( d 2 y d t 2 = − ω 2 y ) . From the given equation, the function is shifted by 1 2 and amplitude is 1 2 , so it oscillates between 0 and 1 . While the oscillatory part is harmonic, the function represents a periodic motion and simple harmonic motion The angular frequency is 2 ω . T = 2 π 2 ω = π ω . Therefore, it is periodic with T = π ω and SHM, i.e., A → III For the displacement equation y = sin 3 ⁡ ( 2 ω t ) Using the identity sin ⁡ 3 θ = 3 sin ⁡ θ − 4 sin 3 ⁡ θ ⇒ sin 3 ⁡ θ = 3 sin ⁡ θ − sin ⁡ 3 θ 4 Here, θ = 2 ω t . So, y = 3 sin ⁡ ( 2 ω t ) − sin ⁡ ( 6 ω t ) 4 = 3 4 sin ⁡ ( 2 ω t ) − 1 4 sin ⁡ ( 6 ω t ) . It is a superposition of two periodic functions y 1 = 3 4 sin ⁡ ( 2 ω t ) and y 2 = − 1 4 sin ⁡ ( 6 ω t ) . The time period is the LCM of time periods of both the periodic functions. The period of first function is T 1 = 2 π / 2 ω = π ω . The period of second function is T 2 = 2 π 6 ω = π 3 ω . So, the time period of function is T = π ω . As it contains multiple frequencies, so it is not SHM. Therefore, it is a periodic function with time period T = π ω but not SHM. ( B → I ) For the displacement equation y = sin ⁡ ( ω t ) + cos ⁡ ( π ω t ) It is a sum of two periodic functions y 1 = sin ⁡ ( ω t ) and y 2 = cos ⁡ ( π ω t ) It will be periodic only if the ratio of their time periods is a rational number. ω 1 = ω ⇒ T 1 = 2 π ω ω 2 = π ω ⇒ T 2 = 2 π π ω = 2 ω Ratio ⁡ T 1 T 2 = 2 π / ω 2 / ω = π Since π is an irrational number, the function is non-periodic. ( C → IV) For the displacement equation y = cos ⁡ ω t + cos ⁡ 2 ω t It is also the sum of two periodic function y 1 = cos ⁡ ω t and y 2 = cos ⁡ 2 ω t ω 1 = ω ⇒ T 1 = 2 π ω and ω 2 = 2 ω ⇒ T 2 = 2 π 2 ω = π ω . The ratio of time periods is, T 1 T 2 = 2 The ratio is rational. The fundamental period is T = 2 π ω . So, it is periodic with time period T = 2 π ω but not SHM ( D → II ) Therefore, the correct match is A - III, B - I, C - IV, D - II. Hence, the correct option is (A).",
