    "subject": "Chemistry",
    "questionEn": "Correct statements from the following are: A. Nitrogen in oxidation states from +1 to +4 disproportionates in acid medium. B. Nitrogen has the ability to form d π - p π multiple bonds with itself and other elements with small size and high electronegativity. C. N-N single bond is stronger than P-P single bond. D. Nitrogen has highest density in its group due to small size. E. The maximum covalency of nitrogen is four since it has only four valence orbitals for bonding. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:",
    "optionsEn": [
      "B, C and D Only",
      "C, D and E Only",
      "A, C and E Only",
      "A and E Only"
    ],
    "correctOptionIndex": 3,
    "explanationEn": "Statement A: Nitrogen in oxidation states from + 1 to + 4 disproportionates in acid medium. This statement is correct. All oxidation states of nitrogen from + 1 to + 4 tend to undergo disproportionation reactions in an acidic solution. For example, nitrous acid, in which nitrogen is in the + 3 oxidation state, disproportionates into nitric acid ( + 5 ) and nitric oxide ( + 2 ): 3 HNO 2 → HNO 3 + H 2 O + 2 NO Statement B: Nitrogen has the ability to form d π - p π multiple bonds with itself and other elements with small size and high electronegativity. This statement is incorrect. The valence shell electronic configuration of nitrogen is 2 s 2 2 p 3 . Since nitrogen belongs to the second period, it does not possess any empty d-orbitals in its valence shell. Due to the absence of d-orbitals, nitrogen can readily form p π - p π multiple bonds, but it is physically impossible for it to form d π - p π bonds. Statement C: N-N single bond is stronger than P-P single bond. This statement is incorrect. The single N-N bond is actually weaker than the single P-P bond. Nitrogen atoms are very small, which results in a short N-N bond length. This brings the non-bonding electron pairs (lone pairs) on the two bonded nitrogen atoms very close to each other, leading to high interelectronic repulsion that weakens the chemical bond. Statement D: Nitrogen has highest density in its group due to small size. This statement is incorrect. As we move down Group 15 from nitrogen to bismuth, the atomic mass increases at a greater rate than the atomic volume. As a result, density smoothly increases down the group. Nitrogen is a diatomic gas at room temperature and has the lowest density in the group, whereas bismuth is a solid and possesses the highest density. Statement E: The maximum covalency of nitrogen is four since it has only four valence orbitals for bonding. This statement is correct. The valence shell of nitrogen consists of only four orbitals available for bonding: one s orbital ( 2 s ) and three p orbitals ( 2 p x , 2 p y , 2 p z ). Without any d-orbitals available to expand its octet, nitrogen is strictly restricted to a maximum covalency of four. Through our step-by-step analysis, we have found that only statements A and E are correct. Correct Answer: Option D (A and E Only)",
