    "correctOptionIndex": 3,
    "explanationEn": "Statement A: Glucose exists in two anomeric forms. This statement is correct. Glucose exists in two different crystalline forms, which are named as the α -form and the β -form. These are known as anomers. Statement B: Anomers of glucose differ in configuration at C − 1 in cyclic hemiacetal structure. This statement is correct. The α and β forms of glucose are cyclic hemiacetals that differ only in the spatial arrangement of the hydroxyl group (-OH) at the first carbon atom ( C − 1 ). This specific carbon is referred to as the anomeric carbon. Statement C: Melting point of α - anomer of glucose is greater than β - anomer. This statement is incorrect. According to experimental data, the melting point of the α -form of glucose is 419 K , whereas the melting point of the β -form is slightly higher, at 423 K . Therefore, the melting point of the β -anomer is greater than that of the α -anomer. Statement D: Specific rotation of α - anomer is + 19 ∘ while for β - anomer is + 112 ∘ This statement is incorrect because the values are interchanged. The specific rotation of the pure α -anomer is + 112.2 ∘ , and the specific rotation of the pure β -anomer is + 19.2 ∘ . Over time, both undergo mutarotation to reach an equilibrium specific rotation of + 52.5 ∘ . Statement E: α and β - anomers of glucose are prepared by crystallization of saturated glucose solution at 303 K and 371 K respectively. This statement is correct. The α -form is typically obtained by the crystallization from a concentrated aqueous solution of glucose at room temperature, around 303 K . On the other hand, the β -form is obtained by crystallizing it from a hot and saturated aqueous solution at 371 K . From the analysis above, the mathematically and factually correct statements are A, B, and E. Therefore, the correct choice is Option D .",
