    "explanationEn": "We need the number of one-one functions f : A → A , where A = { 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 } and the conditions are: f ( 1 ) ≥ 3 f ( 3 ) ≤ 4 f ( 2 ) + f ( 3 ) = 5 Since f is one-one and domain and codomain are the same finite set, such a function is a permutation of A . Now let us count carefully. From f ( 2 ) + f ( 3 ) = 5 and since values are from A = { 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 } , the possible pairs are: ( f ( 2 ) , f ( 3 ) ) = ( 1 , 4 ) , ( 2 , 3 ) , ( 3 , 2 ) , ( 4 , 1 ) Also f ( 3 ) ≤ 4 , which is already satisfied by all these cases. Now we use the condition that f is one-one, so all function values must be distinct. We also have: f ( 1 ) ≥ 3 ⟹ f ( 1 ) ∈ { 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 } But f ( 1 ) must be different from both f ( 2 ) and f ( 3 ) . Case 1: ( f ( 2 ) , f ( 3 ) ) = ( 1 , 4 ) Then f ( 1 ) ∈ { 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 } , but it cannot be 4 because function is one-one. So, f ( 1 ) ∈ { 3 , 5 , 6 } Number of choices for f ( 1 ) = 3 . After fixing f ( 1 ) , f ( 2 ) , f ( 3 ) , the remaining 3 elements of the domain can be mapped to the remaining 3 elements of the codomain in 3 ! = 6 ways. So total in this case: 3 × 6 = 18 Case 2: ( f ( 2 ) , f ( 3 ) ) = ( 2 , 3 ) Then f ( 1 ) ∈ { 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 } , but it cannot be 3 . So, f ( 1 ) ∈ { 4 , 5 , 6 } Number of choices for f ( 1 ) = 3 . Remaining mappings can be done in 3 ! = 6 ways. Total in this case: 3 × 6 = 18 Case 3: ( f ( 2 ) , f ( 3 ) ) = ( 3 , 2 ) Then f ( 1 ) ∈ { 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 } , but it cannot be 3 . So again number of choices for f ( 1 ) = 3 . Remaining mappings: 3 ! = 6 Total: 3 × 6 = 18 Case 4: ( f ( 2 ) , f ( 3 ) ) = ( 4 , 1 ) Then f ( 1 ) ∈ { 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 } , but it cannot be 4 . So number of choices for f ( 1 ) = 3 . Remaining mappings: 3 ! = 6 Total: 3 × 6 = 18 Total number of functions 18 + 18 + 18 + 18 = 72 Hence, the required number of one-one functions is 72",
