    "explanationEn": "The Zeroth Law states that if two systems are each in thermal equilibrium with a third system, they are in thermal equilibrium with each other, i.e., the Zeroth law gives idea about the property of temperature to be measured. Hence, statement A is true. The First Law is the mathematical relation between heat ( Δ Q ), change in internal energy ( Δ U ) and work done by the system ( Δ W ) Δ Q = Δ U + Δ W This relation introduces internal energy ( U ) as a state function which is based on energy conservation. Hence, statement B is true. In an isothermal expansion of an ideal gas, temperature remains constant, so Δ U = 0 . From the First Law: Δ Q = Δ U + Δ W ⇒ Δ Q = 0 + Δ W ⇒ Δ Q = Δ W . But as per statement C , in isothermal process Δ Q ≠ Δ W , so statement C is False. Intensive variables are the properties which do not depend on size, for example temperature and pressure. Extensive variables are the properties depend on size, for example volume. The product of intensive and extensive property P × V represents work or energy, which depends with the size of the system. Therefore, the product is extensive. When an extensive variable (like volume or heat capacity) is divided by mass, it becomes a specific quantity (like specific volume or specific heat). Specific quantities are intensive because they do not depend on the total mass of the system. Statement E says it remains extensive, so it is False. Therefore, only A, B, and D are correct. Hence, the correct option is (C).",
