    "explanationEn": "Tollen’s test: This test helps to identify aldehydes. When an aldehyde reacts with Tollen’s reagent, a silver mirror is formed on the inner walls of the test tube. Hinsberg’s reagent test: This test is used to distinguish different types of amines. The reaction with benzene sulphonyl chloride (Hinsberg’s reagent) gives different products for primary, secondary, and tertiary amines. Lucas test: This test is used to identify alcohols. Alcohols react with Lucas reagent (a mixture of concentrated HCl and anhydrous ZnCl₂), forming turbidity depending on whether the alcohol is primary, secondary, or tertiary. Phthalein dye test: This test is used to detect phenols. Phenols react with phthalic anhydride in the presence of concentrated sulphuric acid to form coloured phthalein dyes.",
