    "explanationEn": "In electrostatic equilibrium, the electric field inside a conductor is zero ( E = 0 ) . According to Gauss's Law ( ∮ E ⋅ dA = q in ε 0 ) , if the electric field is zero everywhere inside, the net charge enclosed ( q in ) by any Gaussian surface inside the conductor must also be zero. Therefore, any excess charge resides entirely on the outer surface. Therefore, assertion A is true. Inside a charged capacitor, there is a uniform electric field between the plates. If free charge carriers (like electrons or ions) are placed in this region, they will experience an electric force ( F = qE ). This force causes the charges to accelerate or drift toward the plate of opposite polarity. Therefore, reason R is true. While both statements are scientifically true, reason R describes the behaviour of charges in an external field between two conductors (the plates). It does not explain why the net charge inside a solid conductor is zero (which is due to mutual repulsion and the resulting zero internal field). So, both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A . Thus, the correct option is (B).",
