    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "Analysis of Statement I: Covalency of Oxygen: The electronic configuration of oxygen is 1 s 2 2 s 2 2 p 4 . In its outermost shell ( n = 2 ), it possesses exactly four orbitals available for bonding: one 2 s orbital and three 2 p orbitals. Because oxygen is a Period 2 element, it completely lacks empty d -orbitals. Consequently, its maximum covalency is restricted to a maximum of 4. While oxygen generally shows a normal covalency of 2 (as seen in H 2 O ), it can exceed this completely up to 4 in certain scenarios (co-ordinating to maximum four atoms as seen theoretically, and a covalency of 3 is commonly observed in the hydronium ion, H 3 O + ). So, the first part of the statement is correct. Oxidation States: In SO 2 , oxygen is more electronegative than sulphur. Here, sulphur has an oxidation state of +4, and each oxygen atom securely takes an oxidation state of -2. In OF 2 , fluorine is the most electronegative element in the periodic table. Because fluorine is more electronegative than oxygen, each fluorine atom takes an oxidation state of -1. To maintain neutrality in the OF 2 molecule, the oxidation state of oxygen must be +2. Therefore, the second part of the statement is perfectly correct as well. Conclusion: Statement I is true. Analysis of Statement II: In the p -block elements, the first member of every group always differs significantly from the heavier members of the same group. According to the NCERT, the anomalous behavior of oxygen compared to the rest of the group 16 elements (like Sulphur, Selenium, etc.) is prominently due to: Its exceptionally small atomic size. Its high electronegativity. The non-availability of d -orbitals in its valence shell. Because of its small size and high electronegativity, it readily forms strong p π − p π double bonds and extensive hydrogen bonds, uniquely distinguishing it from other group elements. The statement accurately lists the main reasons, so it is correct. Conclusion: Statement II is true. Final Conclusion: Since both the provided statements are factually correct based on standard chemical principles, we determine the correct option. Correct Answer: Option A Both Statement I and Statement II are true",
