    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "Let M be the mass of the Earth, R be its radius, and m be the mass of the body ( 1 kg ). At infinity ( r = ∞ ) the potential energy ( U ∞ ) is 0 , and since it falls (starts from rest), the kinetic energy ( K ∞ ) is 0 . Hence, total energy at infinity (initial) is E 1 = K 1 + U 1 = 0 . At Earth's surface ( r = R ) the potential energy is U = − GMm R and the kinetic energy is K = 1 2 mv 2 . Hence, total energy at Earth surface (final) is E 2 = 1 2 mv 2 − GMm R . By conservation of energy ( E 1 = E 2 ) : 0 = 1 2 mv 2 − GMm R ⇒ 1 2 mv 2 = GMm R ⇒ v = √ 2 G M R We know that acceleration due to gravity is g = GM R 2 , so GM = gR 2 . Substituting into the expression for speed, v = √ 2 ( g R 2 ) R = √ 2 g R The acceleration due to gravity at Earth's surface is g = 9.8 m / s 2 and the radius of Earth is R = 6400 km = 6.4 × 10 6 m . Putting the values, v = √ 2 × 9.8 × 6.4 × 10 6 = √ 125.44 × 10 6 ⇒ v = 11.2 × 10 3 m / s = 11.2 km / s The kinetic energy k at the surface is given by : k = 1 2 mv 2 Substituting m = 1 kg and v = 11.2 × 10 3 m / s : k = 1 2 × 1 × ( 11.2 × 10 3 ) 2 ⇒ k = 0.5 × 125.44 × 10 6 ⇒ k = 62.72 × 10 6 J = 6.27 × 10 7 J Hence, at the surface of Earth, the values of v and k respectively are 11.2 km / s , 6.27 × 10 7 J . Hence, the correct option is (A).",
