    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "Using the work-energy theorem, the work done by all forces acting on a body is equal to the change in its kinetic energy. The ball falls from height h = 10 m under gravity. And the ball enters the sand and travels a distance d = 10 cm = 0.1 m before coming to rest. Let m be the mass of the ball, h be the initial height, d be the penetration depth, and F avg be the average resistive force exerted by the sand. The total work done on the ball from the moment it is dropped until it stops is: W total = W gravity + W sand Since the ball starts from rest at height h and ends at rest after depth d , the total change in kinetic energy is zero ( Δ K = 0 ) . W gravity + W sand = 0 Gravity acts over the entire vertical displacement ( h + d ). So, the work done by gravitational force is W gravity = mg ( h + d ) The resistive force F avg acts upwards, opposite to the displacement d . So, the work done by the sand is W sand = − F avg ⋅ d Substituting these into the energy equation : mg ( h + d ) − F avg ⋅ d = 0 ⇒ F avg = mg ( h + d ) d The mass of ball is m = 2 kg , the height of fall is h = 10 m , the distance of penetration in sand is d = 0.1 m , and g = 10 m / s 2 . F avg = 2 × 10 × ( 10 + 0.1 ) 0.1 ⇒ F a v g = 20 × 10.1 0.1 = 20 × 101 ⇒ F avg = 2020 N Therefore, the average force exerted by sand on the ball is 2020 N . Hence, the correct option is (B).",
