    "correctOptionIndex": 3,
    "explanationEn": "Diethyl amine + Ethyl amine (A) : Ethyl amine ( C H 3 C H 2 N H 2 ) is a primary ( 1 ∘ ) aliphatic amine, whereas diethyl amine ( ( C H 3 C H 2 ) 2 N H ) is a secondary ( 2 ∘ ) aliphatic amine. Primary amines answer the Carbylamine test (or isocyanide test). When warmed with chloroform ( C H C l 3 ) and alcoholic potassium hydroxide ( K O H ), primary amines produce a foul-smelling substance called isocyanide. Secondary and tertiary amines do not show this reaction. Therefore, A matches with II. Acetaldehyde + Acetone (B) : Acetaldehyde ( C H 3 C H O ) is an aldehyde, while acetone ( C H 3 C O C H 3 ) is a ketone. Aldehydes can be distinguished from ketones using Tollens' test . When treated with Tollens' reagent (ammoniacal silver nitrate), aldehydes get oxidized to carboxylate ions, and the silver ions are reduced to form a brilliant silver mirror on the inner walls of the test tube. Ketones do not respond to this test. Therefore, B matches with IV. Ethanol + Phenol (C) : Phenol ( C 6 H 5 O H ) has an O H group attached directly to an aromatic ring, which gives a characteristic violet or purple colouration when treated with Neutral F e C l 3 solution due to the formation of a coordination complex. Ethanol ( C H 3 C H 2 O H ), being an aliphatic alcohol, does not react with neutral F e C l 3 . Therefore, C matches with III. Benzoic acid + Cinnamic acid (D) : Cinnamic acid ( C 6 H 5 − C H = C H − C O O H ) is an unsaturated acid as it contains a carbon-carbon double bond in its side chain. It responds to the test for unsaturation and will rapidly decolorize the reddish-brown colour of Bromine water . Benzoic acid ( C 6 H 5 C O O H ) does not have any aliphatic unsaturation and thus will not decolorize bromine water. Therefore, D matches with I. To summarize the correct matching: A → II B → IV C → III D → I Comparing this with the given choices, it corresponds exactly to Option D. Correct Answer: Option D A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I",
