    "explanationEn": "For the quadratic expression a x 2 + 2 √ 2 b x + c to be positive for all x ∈ R , we use the NCERT condition for a quadratic A x 2 + B x + C to satisfy A x 2 + B x + C > 0 for all x ∈ R , which is: A > 0 Discriminant 0 Here, A = a , B = 2 √ 2 b , C = c Since a ∈ { 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 } , we always have a > 0 So we only need D = B 2 − 4 A C 0 Now, D = ( 2 √ 2 b ) 2 − 4 a c = 8 b 2 − 4 a c Thus, 8 b 2 − 4 a c 0 Dividing by 4 : 2 b 2 − a c 0 So the required condition is a c > 2 b 2 Now a , b , c ∈ { 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 } , so total number of choices is 4 × 4 × 4 = 64 We count favourable triples ( a , b , c ) . Case 1: b = 1 Then a c > 2 Now a , c ∈ { 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 } . Total pairs ( a , c ) are 16 . We exclude pairs with a c ≤ 2 : ( 1 , 1 ) gives a c = 1 ( 1 , 2 ) gives a c = 2 ( 2 , 1 ) gives a c = 2 So favourable pairs: 16 − 3 = 13 Case 2: b = 2 Then a c > 8 Possible products greater than 8 are obtained from: ( 3 , 3 ) gives 9 ( 3 , 4 ) gives 12 ( 4 , 3 ) gives 12 ( 4 , 4 ) gives 16 So favourable pairs = 4 . Case 3: b = 3 Then a c > 18 But maximum possible value of a c is 4 ⋅ 4 = 16 So no favourable pair. Case 4: b = 4 Then a c > 32 Again impossible, since maximum a c = 16 . So no favourable pair. Hence total favourable triples are 13 + 4 + 0 + 0 = 17 Therefore, the required probability is 17 64 Thus, m = 17 , n = 64 and m + n = 17 + 64 = 81 So the answer is 81",
