    "explanationEn": "A metal rod of original length L that is free to move. If we heat this rod by a temperature difference Δ T , it will naturally expand. The change in length ( Δ L ) is given by the law of linear thermal expansion : Δ L = L ⋅ α ⋅ Δ T Where α = Coefficient of Linear Expansion When a wire fixed at both ends is cooled, it contracts. Since the supports are rigid, they prevent this contraction, creating a tension in the wire. Strain ε = Δ L L = α Δ T According to Hooke's Law, Stress is proportional to Strain within the elastic limit. The constant of proportionality is Young's Modulus (Y). Y = Stress Strain ⇒ Stress ( σ ) = Y × Strain σ = Y ⋅ ( α Δ T ) Stress is defined as Force (F) per unit Area (A). Stress = F A = Y ⋅ ( α Δ T ) ⇒ F = ( Y α Δ T ) × A ⇒ F ∝ Δ T ⇒ F 1 F 2 = Δ T 1 Δ T 2 Initial Temperature = t 0 = 27 ∘ C Final Temperature = t 1 = − 43 ∘ C Δ T 1 = 27 − ( − 43 ) = 70 ∘ C Initial tension in the wire is, F 1 = T Final tension in the wire is F 2 = 1.4 T Let the new final temperature be t 2 . Δ T 2 = 27 − t 2 ⇒ F 2 F 1 = 1.4 T T = 27 − t 2 70 ⇒ 1.4 = 27 − t 2 70 ⇒ 27 − t 2 = 1.4 × 70 ⇒ 27 − t 2 = 98 ⇒ t 2 = 27 − 98 ⇒ t 2 = − 71 ∘ C",
