    "explanationEn": "Let the mass of the object be m and the initial speed be u . The angle of projection is θ = 60 ∘ . At point A The kinetic energy at point A is given as K . K A = 1 2 mu 2 = K At Point B (Maximum Height) At the highest point of a projectile's path, the vertical component of velocity becomes zero ( v y = 0 ) . The object only possesses the horizontal component of velocity ( v x ). The horizontal velocity remains constant throughout the motion (assuming no air resistance): v B = u x = u cos ⁡ θ ⇒ v B = u cos ⁡ 60 ∘ = u ( 1 2 ) = u 2 So, the kinetic energy at B is, K B = 1 2 m ( v B ) 2 ⇒ K B = 1 2 m ( u 2 ) 2 ⇒ K B = 1 2 m ( u 2 4 ) ⇒ K B = 1 4 ( 1 2 mu 2 ) = 1 4 K A = K 4 ⇒ K B = K 4 At Point C Point C is at the same horizontal level as point A . By the law of conservation of energy, the speed of the projectile when it hits the ground at the same level is equal to the speed of projection. v C = u Therefore, the kinetic energy at C is equal to the kinetic energy at A : K C = K A = K The difference in kinetic energies at B and C : Δ K B C = | K C − K B | = ∣ K − K 4 ∣ = 3 K 4 So, the ratio of the difference in kinetic energies at points B and C to that at point A is Ratio = Δ K B C K A = ( 3 / 4 ) K K = 3 4 Hence, the correct option is (A).",
