    "explanationEn": "The time period (T) of a simple pendulum is the time taken to complete one full oscillation. For a simple pendulum of the length of the string ( L ) and the acceleration due to gravity ( g ) the formula for the time period is, T = 2 π √ L g So, the time period is directly proportional to the square root of the length ( T ∝ √ L ) . T 2 ∝ L ⇒ ∝ T 2 Case - 1 The initial length of the string is L 1 = 30 cm N 1 = 20 is the number of oscillations completed in total time t 1 = 10 s So, the initial time period of oscillation is T 1 = Total Time Number of Oscillations = 10 20 = 0.5 s Case - 2 N 2 = 40 is the number of oscillations completed in the same time t 2 = t 1 = 10 s So, the final time period of oscillation is, T 2 = Total Time Number of Oscillations = 10 40 = 0.25 s We need to find the required length ( L 2 ) corresponding to the final length of the string, Using the proportionality L ∝ T 2 L 2 L 1 = ( T 2 T 1 ) 2 ⇒ L 2 30 = ( 0.25 0.50 ) 2 = 1 4 ⇒ L 2 30 = 1 4 ⇒ L 2 = 30 4 cm = 7.5 cm Therefore, the required length of the string is 7.5 cm . Hence, the correct option is (B).",
