    "subject": "Physics",
    "questionEn": "A wire of uniform resistance λ Ω / m is bent into a circle of radius r and another piece of wire with length 2 r is connected between points A and B ( AOB ) as shown in figure. The equivalent resistance between points A and B is _ _ _ _ Ω .",
    "optionsEn": [
      "( π + 1 ) 2 r λ",
      "6 π λ r 3 π + 16",
      "3 π λ r 8",
      "2 π λ r"
    ],
    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "For the equivalent resistance between points A and B we can identify three distinct paths for the current to flow from node A to node B. These paths are in parallel. The resistance per unit length for the wire is λ Ω / m The resistance is given by R = Resistance per unit length × Length = λ × L . Path 1 (Minor Arc AB): The shorter curved wire along the circle's circumference. Since ∠ AOB = 90 ∘ (a quarter circle), the length is 1 4 of the circumference. L 1 = 1 4 × 2 π r = π r 2 So, the resistance of path 1 is, R 1 = λ ( π r 2 ) Path 2 (Major Arc AOB): The longer curved wire along the remaining part of the circle. This is 3 4 of the circumference. L 2 = 3 4 × 2 π r = 3 π r 2 So, the resistance of path 2 is, R 2 = λ ( 3 π r 2 ) Path 3 (Wire AOB): The straight wire sections connecting A to the centre O and then O to B . Thus, the two segments AO and OB are in series. It is given that the length of this wire is 2 r . L 3 = r + r = 2 r So, the resistance of path 3 is, R 34 = R 3 + R 4 = λ ( 2 r ) Since the three paths are in parallel, the equivalent resistance ( R e q ) is, 1 R eq = 1 R 1 + 1 R 2 + 1 R 34 ⇒ 1 R e q = 1 λ π r / 2 + 1 3 λ π r / 2 + 1 2 λ r ⇒ 1 R e q = 2 λ π r + 2 3 λ π r + 1 2 λ r ⇒ 1 R eq = 1 λ r [ 2 π + 2 3 π + 1 2 ] ⇒ 1 R eq = 1 λ r [ 12 + 4 + 3 π 6 π ] ⇒ 1 R eq = 1 λ r [ 16 + 3 π 6 π ] ⇒ R eq = λ r [ 6 π 16 + 3 π ] = 6 λ π r 3 π + 16 Therefore, the equivalent resistance between points A and B is 6 λ π r 3 π + 16 . Hence, the correct option is (B).",
