    "explanationEn": "Escape velocity ( v e ) is the minimum initial velocity an object needs to completely escape a planet's gravitational field, meaning it reaches an infinite distance with zero remaining kinetic energy. Let an object of mass m be on the surface of a planet with mass M and radius R . Initial Energy at the surface: The object has kinetic energy ( 1 2 mv 2 e ) and gravitational potential energy ( − GMm R ) . Total Initial Energy = 1 2 mv 2 e − GMm R Final Energy at infinity: The object just escapes, meaning it arrives with zero velocity (zero kinetic energy) and at an infinite distance, potential energy is also zero. Total Final Energy = 0 By the law of conservation of energy, Initial Energy = Final Energy: 1 2 m v 2 e − G M m R = 0 ⇒ 1 2 mv 2 e = GMm R v e = √ 2 GM R For a spherical planet, Volume (V) is 4 3 π R 3 . Mass ⁡ ( M ) = Volume ⁡ ( V ) × Density ( ρ ) M = 4 3 π R 3 ρ Substituting this into escape velocity formula : v e = √ 2 G R ⋅ ( 4 3 π R 3 ρ ) ⇒ v e = √ 8 3 π GR 2 ρ ⇒ v e = R √ 8 3 π G ρ ⇒ v e ∝ R √ ρ Escape velocity of planet A , v eA = 10 km / s = 10000 m / s Radius of planet B is 10 % of radius of planet A , R B = 10 % of R A = 0.1 R A Density of planet B is 10 % of that of planet A , ρ B = 10 % of ρ A = 0.1 ρ A Using proportionality relationship, v eB v eA = R B √ ρ B R A √ ρ A ⇒ v eB v eA = 0.1 R A √ 0.1 ρ A R A √ ρ A ⇒ v eB v eA = 0.1 √ 0.1 ⇒ v eB v eA = 1 10 × 1 √ 10 = 1 10 √ 10 ⇒ v eB = 10000 × ( 1 10 √ 10 ) v eB = 1000 √ 10 = 100 √ 10 m / s Therefore, the escape velocity of another planet B is 100 √ 10 m / s . Hence, the correct option is (D).",
