    "explanationEn": "Newton's Law of Universal Gravitation states that every point mass attracts every other point mass by a force acting along the line intersecting both points. The magnitude of this force is, F = Gm 1 m 2 r 2 where G is the gravitational constant, m 1 and m 2 are the masses, and r is the distance between their centers. Let's consider square of side length a and the distance from any corner to the centre be r . Let's place a test mass m 0 at the center. The gravitational force exerted by a mass m at a corner on this test mass will be directed towards that corner with a magnitude: F = Gm ⋅ m 0 r 2 Let the force on m 0 by another mass M is F 0 = GM ⋅ m 0 r 2 So, a mass of 2 M will exert a force of 2 F 0 , 3 M will exert 3 F 0 , and 4 M will exert 4 F 0 . Case 1 : Initial arrangement The magnitudes of forces on the mass kept at centre of square are; F 1 = 4 F 0 , F 2 = 3 F 0 , F 3 = 2 F 0 and F 4 = F 0 Since F 2 and F 4 pull in opposite directions, the net force along this diagonal is 3 F 0 − F 0 = 2 F 0 directed along F 2 . The net force of F 1 and F 3 is along F 1 is 4 F 0 − 2 F 0 = 2 F 0 . Now two net forces, each of magnitude 2 F 0 , acting at a 90 ∘ angle to each other. Then the resultant magnitude ( F ) 1 : ( F ) 1 = √ ( 2 F 0 ) 2 + ( 2 F 0 ) 2 = √ 8 F 2 0 ⇒ ( F ) 1 = 2 √ 2 F 0 Case 2 : Positions of 3 M and 4 M interchanged The magnitudes of forces on the mass kept at centre of square are; F 1 = 4 F 0 , F 2 = 3 F 0 , F 3 = 2 F 0 and F 4 = F 0 Since F 2 and F 3 pull in opposite directions, the net force along this diagonal is 3 F 0 − 2 F 0 = F 0 directed along F 2 . The net force of F 1 and F 4 is along F 1 is 4 F 0 − F 0 = 3 F 0 . Now two net forces are acting at a 90 ∘ angle to each other. Then the resultant magnitude ( F ) 2 : ( F ) 2 = √ ( F 0 ) 2 + ( 3 F 0 ) 2 = √ 10 F 2 0 ⇒ ( F ) 2 = √ 10 F 0 The ratio ( F ) 1 ( F ) 2 is : ( F ) 1 ( F ) 2 = 2 √ 2 F 0 √ 10 F 0 = 2 √ 5 ⇒ ⇒ 2 √ 5 = α √ 5 ⇒ α = 2 Therefore, the value of α is 2 . Hence, the correct option is (A).",
