    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "According to Faraday's Law of Electromagnetic Induction, the induced emf ⁡ ( ε ) is the negative rate of change of magnetic flux ( φ ) through the loop : ε = − d φ dt When the field is perpendicular to the plane of the loop then magnetic flux is φ = B ⋅ A The area of the loop is A = 1.0 m 2 The magnetic field in the region is B = sin ⁡ ( 100 t ) So, the magnetic flux is, φ = 1.0 sin ⁡ ( 100 t ) = sin ⁡ ( 100 t ) Now on differentiating the flux with respect to time ( t ) we get the induced emf : ε = − d d t [ sin ⁡ ( 100 t ) ] ⇒ ε = − 100 cos ⁡ ( 100 t ) Volts The instantaneous power ( P ) dissipated as heat in a resistor is: P = ε 2 R Given resistance of the loop is R = 100 Ω : P = [ − 100 cos ⁡ ( 100 t ) ] 2 100 ⇒ P = 10000 cos 2 ⁡ ( 100 t ) 100 = 100 cos 2 ⁡ ( 100 t ) Watts The average thermal energy dissipated in one period is equivalent to finding the average power over one full cycle ( T ) in time T . The time period ( T ) for the function sin ⁡ ( 100 t ) is, T = 2 π ω = 2 π 100 = π 50 seconds The total energy (E) dissipated in one period is : E = ∫ T 0 Pdt = ∫ T 0 ( 100 cos 2 ⁡ ( 100 t ) ) dt ⇒ E = 100 ∫ T 0 cos 2 ⁡ ( 100 t ) dt = 100 ∫ T 0 1 + cos ⁡ ( 200 t ) 2 dt ⇒ E = 50 ∫ T 0 1 + cos ⁡ ( 200 t ) d t ⇒ E = 50 [ t + sin ⁡ ( 200 t ) 200 ] π / 50 0 ⇒ E = 50 [ ( π 50 + sin ⁡ ( 200 × π 50 ) 200 ) − ( 0 + 0 ) ] ⇒ E = 50 [ π 50 + sin ⁡ ( 4 π ) 200 ] ⇒ E = 50 × π 50 = π Joules",
