    "explanationEn": "For a sparingly soluble salt AB 2 , the dissolution in water can be represented by the following equilibrium equation: AB 2 ⇌ A 2 + + 2 B − When AB 2 dissolves in water, it generates one A 2 + ion and two B − ions. The solubility product constant, K s p , for this reaction can be expressed as: K s p = [ A 2 + ] [ B − ] 2 Given in the problem, the equilibrium concentrations are: [ A 2 + ] = 1.2 × 10 − 4 M [ B − ] = 0.24 × 10 − 3 M = 2.4 × 10 − 4 M Substituting these values into the K s p expression: K s p = ( 1.2 × 10 − 4 ) × ( 2.4 × 10 − 4 ) 2 Calculating K s p : K s p = 1.2 × 10 − 4 × ( 5.76 × 10 − 8 ) K s p = 6.912 × 10 − 12 Therefore, the correct option is: Option C: 6.91 × 10 − 12",
