    "explanationEn": "To determine the coefficient of kinetic friction, let's analyze the time taken by the object to slide down each plane and use the equations of motion for both scenarios. First, consider the perfectly smooth 45 ∘ inclined plane (no friction). The acceleration of the object on this plane can be calculated using the component of gravitational force parallel to the incline. Since there is no friction, the only force acting down the plane is the component of the gravitational force: a smooth = g sin ⁡ 45 ∘ = g √ 2 Let the time taken to slide down this smooth plane be t smooth . The distance d covered by the object can be expressed using the equation of motion: d = 1 2 a smooth t 2 smooth = 1 2 g √ 2 t 2 smooth Now, consider the rough 45 ∘ inclined plane. The acceleration down the rough plane can be found by considering both the component of gravitational force and the kinetic friction force. Here, the friction force is f k = μ k N , where N = m g cos ⁡ 45 ∘ = m g √ 2 . Thus the frictional force is: f k = μ k ⋅ m g √ 2 The net force acting on the object down the plane would be: F net = m g sin ⁡ 45 ∘ − f k = m g √ 2 − μ k ⋅ m g √ 2 So the net acceleration a rough is: a rough = F net m = g √ 2 − μ k ⋅ g √ 2 = g √ 2 ( 1 − μ k ) The time taken to slide down the rough plane is given as n t smooth . So, the distance d can be written as: d = 1 2 a rough ( n t smooth ) 2 = 1 2 ( g √ 2 ( 1 − μ k ) ) ( n t smooth ) 2 Setting the distances equal for both cases, we get: 1 2 g √ 2 t 2 smooth = 1 2 ( g √ 2 ( 1 − μ k ) ) ( n t smooth ) 2 Simplifying, we find: t 2 smooth = ( 1 − μ k ) n 2 t 2 smooth Therefore: 1 = ( 1 − μ k ) n 2 Solving for μ k , we get: μ k = 1 − 1 n 2 Thus, the coefficient of kinetic friction between the object and the surface of inclined plane is: Option A: 1 − 1 n 2",
