    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "First, let us determine the d -electron count of the cobalt center in [ Co ( H 2 O ) 6 ] 3 + . 1. Oxidation state and d -electron count Neutral cobalt (Co) has an atomic number of 27 and an electronic configuration: Co [ Ar ] 3 d 7 4 s 2 . In the +3 oxidation state , cobalt has lost a total of 3 electrons: First two electrons are removed from the 4 s orbital. The third electron is removed from the 3 d orbitals. Thus, Co 3 + has 3 d 7 − 1 = 3 d 6 . So in [ Co ( H 2 O ) 6 ] 3 + , the cobalt center is d 6 . 2. High-spin vs Low-spin for Co 3 + Even though H 2 O is generally considered a weak field ligand, the Co 3 + ion has a relatively high charge (+3). A higher charge on the metal center usually increases the ligand-field splitting ( Δ o ) significantly compared to lower oxidation states of the same metal. In most typical octahedral complexes of Co 3 + , the splitting Δ o is large enough that the complex ends up being low spin . Electronic configuration in an octahedral field For a d 6 octahedral low-spin complex, all six electrons pair up in the lower-energy t 2 g orbitals: t 6 2 g e 0 g That leaves 0 unpaired electrons. 3. Final answer Therefore, the number of unpaired d -electrons in [ Co ( H 2 O ) 6 ] 3 + is 0 . Correct Option: A (0)",
