    "explanationEn": "An amphoteric oxide is one that displays both acidic and basic properties; such substances react with both acids and bases. In the p-block, several elements can form amphoteric oxides, particularly those elements that are metallic or metalloid in nature. It's essential to recognize which oxides among the provided list have an amphoteric character: Cl 2 O 7 - This is dichlorine heptoxide, an oxide of chlorine, and shows acidic properties. It is not amphoteric. CO - Carbon monoxide does not behave as an acidic or basic oxide; it is mainly a neutral oxide. PbO 2 - Lead(IV) oxide, also known as plumbic oxide. This oxide of lead is amphoteric, but it is much less common and less amphoteric than lead(II) oxide, PbO. N 2 O - Nitrous oxide is a neutral oxide; it does not exhibit acidic or basic properties. NO - Nitric oxide, like N2O, is a neutral oxide. Al 2 O 3 - Aluminium oxide is well known for being amphoteric. It reacts with both acids and bases to produce salts and water. SiO 2 - Silicon dioxide is generally acidic. Although it does not react with water to form an acidic solution, it will react with basic oxides. Therefore, it is typically not considered amphoteric. N 2 O 5 - Dinitrogen pentoxide is an acidic oxide; it is the anhydride of nitric acid. SnO 2 - Tin dioxide, also known as stannic oxide. This oxide of tin is amphoteric, reacting with both acids and bases. From the list, the amphoteric oxides are: PbO 2 - Lead(IV) oxide (less commonly amphoteric compared to PbO) Al 2 O 3 - Aluminium oxide SnO 2 - Tin dioxide So, the number of amphoteric oxides in the list provided is 3.",
