    "subject": "Physics",
    "questionEn": "The pressure and volume of an ideal gas are related as PV 3 2 = K (Constant). The work done when the gas is taken from state A ( P 1 , V 1 , T 1 ) to state B ( P 2 , V 2 , T 2 ) is :",
    "optionsEn": [
      "2 ( P 2 √ V 2 − P 1 √ V 1 )",
      "2 ( √ P 1 V 1 − √ P 2 V 2 )",
      "2 ( P 2 V 2 − P 1 V 1 )",
      "2 ( P 1 V 1 − P 2 V 2 )"
    ],
    "correctOptionIndex": 3,
    "explanationEn": "To find the work done by the gas when it goes from state A to state B, we can look at the definition of work done on or by a gas in a thermodynamic process. For a quasi-static process, the work done W is given by the integral of the pressure P with respect to the volume V : W = ∫ V 2 V 1 P d V Given the relationship P V 3 2 = K , we can solve for P : P = K V 3 2 Now, substitute P into the work integral and evaluate it: W = ∫ V 2 V 1 K V 3 2 d V = K ∫ V 2 V 1 V − 3 2 d V To integrate this, we'll use the power rule for integration. The integral of V − 3 2 is − 2 V − 1 2 , so the work done is: W = K [ − 2 V − 1 2 ] V 2 V 1 = K ( − 2 V − 1 2 2 + 2 V − 1 2 1 ) We can rewrite V − 1 2 as 1 √ V : W = K ( − 2 1 √ V 2 + 2 1 √ V 1 ) Since P 2 V 3 2 2 = K and P 1 V 3 2 1 = K , we can express K in terms of P 1 and V 1 (or P 2 and V 2 , but we’ll use P 1 and V 1 for now): W = P 1 V 3 2 1 ( − 2 1 √ V 2 + 2 1 √ V 1 ) W = − 2 P 1 V 1 √ V 1 1 √ V 2 + 2 P 1 V 1 √ V 1 1 √ V 1 W = − 2 P 1 V 1 √ V 1 √ V 2 + 2 P 1 V 1 Since √ V 1 √ V 2 = √ V 1 V 2 , and recalling P 2 V 3 2 2 = K once more: W = − 2 P 1 V 1 √ V 1 V 2 + 2 P 1 V 1 W = − 2 P 1 V 3 2 1 √ V 2 + 2 P 1 V 1 Putting P 1 V 3 2 1 back as K : W = − 2 K √ V 2 + 2 P 1 V 1 W = − 2 P 2 V 2 √ V 2 1 √ V 2 + 2 P 1 V 1 W = − 2 P 2 V 2 + 2 P 1 V 1 So the work done is: W = 2 P 1 V 1 − 2 P 2 V 2 Therefore, the correct answer matching the given options is: Option D: 2 ( P 1 V 1 − P 2 V 2 ) Shortcut : For PV x = constant If work done by gas is asked then W = nR Δ T 1 − x Here x = 3 2 ∴ W = P 2 V 2 − P 1 V 1 − 1 2 = 2 ( P 1 V 1 − P 2 V 2 )",
