    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "Adsorption chromatography separates the components of a mixture by their differential adsorption on a solid adsorbent (the stationary phase). In column chromatography the mixture moves down a column packed with an adsorbent such as alumina or silica gel; components adsorbed less strongly move faster, so they separate. Thin layer chromatography uses a thin layer of adsorbent (silica gel or alumina) spread on a glass plate, so it is also adsorption chromatography. Paper chromatography is partition chromatography: water held by the cellulose of the paper is the stationary phase, and separation depends on differential partitioning between this water and the mobile phase. So only column chromatography and thin layer chromatography (A and B) are based on differential adsorption.",
