    "explanationEn": "The correct set of four quantum numbers for the valence electron of a rubidium atom ( Z = 37 ) is: Principal quantum number ( n ): This indicates the main energy level or shell of the electron. For rubidium, the valence electron is in the 5th energy level, so n = 5 . Azimuthal quantum number ( l ): This defines the subshell or orbital type. The possible values for l range from 0 to n − 1 . For rubidium, the valence electron is in the 5 s orbital, so l = 0 . Magnetic quantum number ( m l ): This indicates the orientation of the orbital in space. For l = 0 , the only possible value is m l = 0 . Spin quantum number ( m s ): This represents the spin of the electron, which can be either + 1 2 or − 1 2 . Generally, when an electron is added to an unoccupied orbital, it starts with a spin of + 1 2 . Therefore, the correct set of quantum numbers for the valence electron of rubidium is: Option B: 5 , 0 , 0 , + 1 2",
