    "explanationEn": "Let's denote the constant acceleration with which the body moves as a . We know that the displacement S covered by a body starting from rest under a constant acceleration a in time t is given by the equation of motion: S = 1 2 a t 2 . Considering the displacement S 1 in first ( p − 1 ) seconds, we apply the equation of motion: S 1 = 1 2 a ( p − 1 ) 2 Similarly, for the displacement S 2 in first p seconds: S 2 = 1 2 a p 2 To find out the total time it will take to cover the displacement S 1 + S 2 , we first find the sum of these two displacements: S 1 + S 2 = 1 2 a ( p − 1 ) 2 + 1 2 a p 2 Let's simplify this: S 1 + S 2 = 1 2 a ( ( p − 1 ) 2 + p 2 ) S 1 + S 2 = 1 2 a ( p 2 − 2 p + 1 + p 2 ) S 1 + S 2 = 1 2 a ( 2 p 2 − 2 p + 1 ) S 1 + S 2 = a ( 2 p 2 − 2 p + 1 2 ) If we consider the total displacement S 1 + S 2 is to be covered in a time t seconds from rest, we should set this equal to the equation of motion: S 1 + S 2 = 1 2 a t 2 Equating the two equations: a ( 2 p 2 − 2 p + 1 2 ) = 1 2 a t 2 Since a ≠ 0 , we can simplify by dividing both sides by 1 2 a : 2 p 2 − 2 p + 1 2 = t 2 2 2 p 2 − 2 p + 1 = t 2 To find t , we take the square root of both sides: t = √ 2 p 2 − 2 p + 1 Therefore, the time taken to cover the displacement S 1 + S 2 will be: t = √ ( 2 p 2 − 2 p + 1 ) s Hence, the correct option would be: Option D: √ ( 2 p 2 − 2 p + 1 ) s",
