    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "First, we need to determine the amount of O 2 (oxygen gas) in moles that is displaced by the quantity of electricity mentioned. To do this, we'll use the molar volume of a gas at Standard Temperature and Pressure (S.T.P.), which is approximately 22.4 L / mol (or 22400 mL / mol ). The volume of O 2 is given as 5600 mL . Now, we convert this volume to moles: moles of O 2 = volume of O 2 molar volume = 5600 mL 22400 mL / mol = 0.25 mol Next, we'll use Faraday's laws of electrolysis to relate the moles of O 2 to the moles of silver being displaced. In electrolysis, silver is deposited at the cathode according to the following half-reaction: Ag + + e − → Ag This tells us that for each mole of Ag + ions, only 1 mole of electrons is required to reduce it to silver metal Ag . However, the liberation of oxygen gas involves the following half-reaction: 2 H 2 O ( l ) → O 2 ( g ) + 4 H + ( a q ) + 4 e − From this reaction, we can see that 1 mole of O 2 gas requires 4 moles of electrons to be produced. Therefore, the number of moles of electrons associated with the 0.25 mol of O 2 will be: moles of electrons for O 2 = 0.25 mol × 4 = 1 mol Now, since it takes 1 mole of electrons to reduce 1 mole of Ag + . The moles of electrons required is equal to the moles of Ag produced. Hence, we have 1 mole of Ag being deposited. Finally, to calculate the mass of this silver, we use the molar mass of silver: Mass of Ag = ( moles of Ag ) × ( Molar mass of Ag ) = 1 mol × 108 gmol − 1 = 108 g So, the mass of silver displaced by the quantity of electricity that displaces 5600 mL of O 2 at S.T.P. will be 108 g .",
