    "explanationEn": "The process involves an ideal gas expanding isothermally, meaning temperature ( T ) remains constant. In an isothermal process for an ideal gas, the change in internal energy ( Δ U ) is zero: Δ U = 0 because temperature is constant. According to the first law of thermodynamics, Δ U = Q + W , where Q is the heat transferred to the system and W is the work done by the system. Since Δ U = 0 in an isothermal process, Q = − W . The work done by the gas during the isothermal expansion against a constant external pressure ( P e x t ) is given by: W = P e x t × Δ V where: P e x t = constant opposing pressure = 80 kPa = 80 × 10 3 Pa (since 1 kPa = 10 3 Pa) Δ V = change in volume = 45 dm³ - 30 dm³ = 15 dm³ = 15 × 10 − 3 m³ (since 1 dm³ = 10 − 3 m³) W = − 80 × 10 3 × 15 × 10 − 3 = − 1200 J Therefore, the amount of heat transferred ( Q ) is 1200 J, taking into consideration the sign convention that work done by the system is negative.",
