    "explanationEn": "Given, points P and R are on the ellipse defined by 9 x 2 + 4 y 2 = 36 which simplifies to x 2 4 + y 2 9 = 1 . This is the standard form of the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin, with semi-major axis a = 3 along the y -axis and semi-minor axis b = 2 along the x -axis. OP is the distance from origin O to point P, which is given by : O P = r 1 = √ ( 2 √ 3 √ 7 ) 2 + ( 6 √ 7 ) 2 = √ 12 7 + 36 7 = √ 48 7 = 2 √ 12 7 . 1. Let's represent the given point P ( 2 √ 3 √ 7 , 6 √ 7 ) in polar coordinates. We can write P as ( r 1 cos ⁡ θ , r 1 sin ⁡ θ ) . Since P lies on the ellipse, it must satisfy the equation of the ellipse. Substituting x = r 1 cos ⁡ θ and y = r 1 sin ⁡ θ into the equation of the ellipse gives us : r 2 1 cos 2 ⁡ θ 4 + r 2 1 sin 2 ⁡ θ 9 = 1 Simplifying this, we obtain : cos 2 ⁡ θ 4 + sin 2 ⁡ θ 9 = 7 48 --- (equation 1) 2. Similarly, if we represent the point R as ( − r 2 sin ⁡ θ , r 2 cos ⁡ θ ) , (the negative sign is due to the fact that line RS is perpendicular to line PQ. Since they are perpendicular, the angle between them is 90 degrees or π / 2 radians. In terms of sin and cos, sin ⁡ ( θ + π / 2 ) = cos ⁡ ( θ ) and cos ⁡ ( θ + π / 2 ) = − sin ⁡ ( θ ) .) it too should satisfy the equation of the ellipse. We have : r 2 2 sin 2 ⁡ θ 4 + r 2 2 cos 2 ⁡ θ 9 = 1 Simplifying this, we obtain : sin 2 ⁡ θ 4 + cos 2 ⁡ θ 9 = 1 r 2 2 --- (equation 2) 3. From equations (1) and (2), we have : 1 r 2 2 = 1 4 + 1 9 − 7 48 = 31 144 4. Now, note that lines P Q and R S are perpendicular and pass through the origin, so P Q = 2 O P and R S = 2 O R . Thus, 1 P Q 2 + 1 R S 2 = 1 4 ( 1 r 2 1 + 1 r 2 2 ) 5. Substituting the values of r 1 and r 2 , we obtain : 1 P Q 2 + 1 R S 2 = 1 4 ( 7 48 + 31 144 ) = 13 144 = p q 6. Hence, p = 13 and q = 144 . 7. So, the final answer p + q = 13 + 144 = 157 .",
