    "explanationEn": "To solve the integral: I = ∫ [ ( x 2 ) x + ( 2 x ) x ] ln ⁡ ( e x 2 ) d x we start by simplifying the logarithmic term: ln ⁡ ( e x 2 ) = ln ⁡ ( e ) + ln ⁡ ( x 2 ) = 1 + ln ⁡ ( x 2 ) So the integral becomes: I = ∫ [ ( x 2 ) x + ( 2 x ) x ] [ 1 + ln ⁡ ( x 2 ) ] d x Let's denote: A = ( x 2 ) x B = ( 2 x ) x Then, the integral can be split: I = ∫ [ A ( 1 + ln ⁡ ( x 2 ) ) + B ( 1 + ln ⁡ ( x 2 ) ) ] d x Calculating ∫ A ( 1 + ln ⁡ ( x 2 ) ) d x : First, find the derivative of A : d A d x = A [ ln ⁡ ( x 2 ) + 1 ] This means: A [ 1 + ln ⁡ ( x 2 ) ] = d A d x Therefore: ∫ A [ 1 + ln ⁡ ( x 2 ) ] d x = ∫ d A d x d x = A + C 1 = ( x 2 ) x + C 1 Calculating ∫ B ( 1 + ln ⁡ ( x 2 ) ) d x : Note that: ln ⁡ ( x 2 ) = ln ⁡ x − ln ⁡ 2 ln ⁡ ( 2 x ) = ln ⁡ 2 − ln ⁡ x The derivative of B is: d B d x = B [ ln ⁡ ( 2 x ) + 1 ] = B [ ln ⁡ 2 − ln ⁡ x + 1 ] But in our integrand, we have B [ 1 + ln ⁡ ( x 2 ) ] = B [ 1 + ln ⁡ x − ln ⁡ 2 ] . Notice that: 1 + ln ⁡ x − ln ⁡ 2 = − ( ln ⁡ 2 − ln ⁡ x + 1 ) Thus: B [ 1 + ln ⁡ ( x 2 ) ] = − d B d x Therefore: ∫ B [ 1 + ln ⁡ ( x 2 ) ] d x = − ∫ d B d x d x = − B + C 2 = − ( 2 x ) x + C 2 Combining the Results: Adding both integrals: I = ( x 2 ) x − ( 2 x ) x + C Answer: Option B ( x 2 ) x − ( 2 x ) x + C",
