    "subject": "Chemistry",
    "questionEn": "Consider the following reaction approaching equilibrium at 27 ∘ C and 1 atm pressure A + B ⇌ k r = 10 2 k f = 10 3 C + D The standard Gibb's energy change ( Δ r G θ ) at 27 ∘ C is ( − ) ___________ kJ mol − 1 (Nearest integer). (Given : R = 8.3 J K − 1 mol − 1 and ln ⁡ 10 = 2.3 )",
    "optionsEn": [
      "Value 1",
      "Value 2",
      "Value 3",
      "Value 4"
    ],
    "correctOptionIndex": 0,
    "explanationEn": "The Gibbs energy change for a reaction at standard conditions, Δ r G θ , can be calculated using the equation: Δ G θ = − RT ln ⁡ K eq Where K eq is the equilibrium constant given by K f K b . For the reaction: A + B ⇌ C + D The forward rate constant, k f , is 10 3 , and the reverse rate constant, k r , is 10 2 . Therefore, K eq = 10 3 10 2 = 10 Substitute the values into the Gibbs energy change formula: Δ G θ = − RT ln ⁡ 10 where R = 8.3 J K − 1 mol − 1 and T = 300 K (since the temperature is 27 ∘ C which converts to 300 K). The natural logarithm of 10 is approximately 2.3. Δ G θ = − ( 8.3 × 300 × 2.3 ) Calculating gives: Δ G θ = − 5727 J mol − 1 Converting to kJ: Δ G θ = − 5.7 kJ mol − 1 Rounding to the nearest integer, the standard Gibb's energy change is approximately − 6 kJ mol − 1 .",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "tags": [
      "Chemistry",
      "JEE Main 2023",
      "January Session"
    ]
